## Computational consequence

The earlier `II -> HI -> HH` potential proves that domination atoms alone are order-acyclic. A useful order contradiction therefore combines domination choices with independently forced segment comparisons from the affine cell or physical branch.

The present theorem makes that interaction pairwise. After exact forced-sign extraction on a surviving H6 branch, a complete minimal-cycle cut generator only needs:

1. single domination literals from the fourteen clauses;
2. the 42 cross-target, distinct-root clause pairs above;
3. at branch-atom resolution, only the 420 static aligned atom pairs before applying the much smaller state-specific bracketing set.

The attempted same-root target-`6`/target-`7` depth-two audit cannot produce a *two-domination-literal order-cycle* certificate: same-root clause pairs are absent from the 42-pair list. That audit is still mathematically relevant for joint affine feasibility, because an affine Farkas contradiction need not be an order cycle.

Accordingly, the next order-layer implementation should exactify forced signs among the 168 rooted segment comparisons and preindex the 8,242 minimal templates by zero, one, or two aligned domination literals. It can emit unary and binary forbidden branch assignments before invoking the full fourteen-disjunction affine BnB or nonlinear sine-cycle checks.

This is a strict reduction from considering arbitrary fourteen-variable domination interactions in the segment-order layer to a finite unary/binary cut system.

## Scope

This theorem does not remove an H6 cell by itself, does not prove joint affine feasibility of the domination clauses, and does not change the current exact 3,874-cell generic-H6 endpoint. It applies only to minimal `L(K8)` segment-order cycle certificates. Reciprocal-collinearity walls remain separate. C-II remains open.
